# Copy to .env beside this file, and fill in:
#     cp atriumdb_dashboard/.env.example atriumdb_dashboard/.env
#
# config.py also accepts sdk/.env (the working directory), but beside this file
# is preferred: it is found relative to the package rather than to whatever
# directory the process was started from, so it keeps working if uvicorn is ever
# launched from somewhere else.
#
# Both locations are gitignored (.gitignore line 149 is a bare ".env", which git
# applies at any depth) and both are excluded from the Docker build context by
# docker/Dockerfile.dockerignore. Never commit real values, and never put a
# secret in THIS file.
#
# Precedence: a real environment variable always beats this file. The dashboard
# loads it with override=False, so a deployment that injects variables directly
# is unaffected by a stray .env. In production prefer injecting the variables
# (docker run --env-file, compose env_file:, or a secret store) over shipping a
# file.

# ===========================================================================
# Metadata backend  ->  get_meta_sdk
# Serves POST /cohorts and GET /measures/hours, which run raw SQL.
# ===========================================================================

# "sqlite" (default, local development) or "mariadb" (UAT and production).
ATRIUMDB_METADATA_CONNECTION_TYPE=sqlite

# In sqlite mode: the dataset directory holding meta/index.db and tsc/.
#
# In mariadb mode: still REQUIRED, but only to satisfy the SDK constructor,
# which builds a file handler before it looks at the connection type. The
# metadata SDK never reads a .tsc file through it. Any existing directory works.
ATRIUMDB_DATASET_LOCATION=/data/atriumdb

# --- mariadb mode only -----------------------------------------------------
# Ask for a READ-ONLY account. Nothing the dashboard does writes, and a
# read-only grant turns any accidental auto_upgrade=True into a permissions
# error rather than a schema migration on a shared server.
#
# Tables read: settings, measure, block_index, encounter, bed, unit, patient,
# patient_history.
# ATRIUMDB_MARIA_HOST=
# ATRIUMDB_MARIA_PORT=3306
# ATRIUMDB_MARIA_USER=
# ATRIUMDB_MARIA_PASSWORD=          # secret
# ATRIUMDB_MARIA_DATABASE=

# ===========================================================================
# Data backend  ->  get_data_sdk
# Serves POST /cohorts/statistics and POST /cohorts/timeseries.
# Leave ATRIUMDB_API_URL unset for a purely local deployment: those two
# endpoints then return 503 and the other two are unaffected.
# ===========================================================================

# Where the AtriumDB API actually listens. A DIFFERENT value from
# AUTH0_AUDIENCE, and it has to come from the provider.
#
# Include the FULL prefix, version segment and all. The SDK appends bare
# endpoint names ("measures/", "sdk/blocks") to this, and there is no separate
# setting for a version prefix, so a versioned API must carry it here or every
# request 404s:
#
#   docs at https://host/api/docs, routes like /v1/measures
#     ->  ATRIUMDB_API_URL=https://host/api/v1
#
# No trailing slash.
# ATRIUMDB_API_URL=https://host/api/v1

# --- Auth0 machine-to-machine ----------------------------------------------
# Tenant hostname. Bare host or full URL both work; http:// is upgraded to
# https:// because Auth0 rejects plaintext with HTTP 426.
# AUTH0_TENANT=your-tenant.us.auth0.com

# AUTH0_CLIENT_ID=
# AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET=              # secret

# Which API the token is minted FOR. An Auth0 identifier, not necessarily a
# reachable URL -- it is stamped into the token's "aud" claim and checked by the
# API server. A mismatch here is the usual cause of a 401. NOT the same thing as
# ATRIUMDB_API_URL, though convention often makes them look similar.
# AUTH0_AUDIENCE=

# Only client_credentials is supported. Auth0 issues no refresh token for it, so
# the dashboard mints one token per process and re-mints near expiry.
AUTH0_GRANT_TYPE=client_credentials

# Optional. A pre-minted token, which skips the Auth0 round trip while
# iterating. Its real expiry still governs, so a stale one is not used forever.
#   export ATRIUMDB_API_TOKEN=$(python3 remote/auth0_token.py)
# ATRIUMDB_API_TOKEN=

# ===========================================================================
# Logging
# ===========================================================================

# INFO by default. DEBUG surfaces per-entry diagnostics including the full value
# grid each resolver fetches -- roughly 1 MB per patient per request, so useful
# for one patient and very large for a cohort.
# ATRIUMDB_DASHBOARD_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Optional path for the exclusion audit trail. When set, exclusion records go
# there instead of the console. These records carry MRNs, so the destination
# needs the same handling as any other file holding patient identifiers.
# ATRIUMDB_DASHBOARD_EXCLUSION_LOG=/var/log/atriumdb/exclusions.log
